1: # Plan 6: command-specific ENHANCE preparation
2: 
3: Status: proposed next capability slice; two fresh adversarial reviews required
4: before implementation. The nonanchored unbounded gate must finish first.
5: 
6: ## Problem
7: 
8: Prepared execution currently rejects every per-dab host hook and every read-only
9: attribute except the boundary class. ENHANCE needs a host-generated displacement
10: field. Its raw hook reads enhance_rings and enhance_inner from Brush, then caches
11: one vector per vertex under strokeGen alone. Two commands with different settings
12: therefore cannot share that cache. Deduplicating hook function pointers also loses
13: command-specific execution order and settings.
14: 
15: ## Implementation proposal
16: 
17: 1. Add authoritative static INT32 snapshots for enhance_rings and enhance_inner,
18:    using the existing executor-setting machinery and native descriptors. Permit
19:    command scalar overrides but no device stack until dynamic eligibility is
20:    explicitly defined. Preserve the kernel's existing normalization:
21:    outer = max(rings, 1), inner = clamp(inner, 0, outer). Validate any arithmetic
22:    bounds required to avoid signed overflow; do not invent an artist-facing cap.
23:    The source comments incorrectly say inner must be strictly less than outer;
24:    actual native behavior allows equality and yields zero detail.
25: 2. Classify prepared hooks as invariant preparation or command-specific per-dab
26:    preparation. Continue deduplicating only the invariant boundary refresh.
27:    Invoke ENHANCE preparation after applying that command's prepared values and
28:    immediately before its kernel, with the program's live-topology requirement
29:    computed across all commands before mutation. Preserve raw execution policy.
30: 3. Add an executor-owned prepared ENHANCE cache keyed by normalized outer/inner,
31:    mesh identity and topology generation, reset at stroke boundaries. Store
32:    first-contact vectors per key and vertex plus a reusable active column.
33:    Each stage claims vertices inside its own effective support, even when a
34:    larger/unbounded stage widens the shared node list. With nonaccumulating
35:    kernels, use the same base-coordinate support convention as prepared cavity.
36:    Compute the vector from the live geometry/normal at that first contact, using
37:    the original difference-of-smooths formula and reusable BFS scratch.
38: 4. Before each ENHANCE kernel, install that configuration's active values into
39:    the existing temporary displacement attribute or an equivalent scoped binding.
40:    Admit exactly its vertex/FLOAT3/read-only/no-extra-use metadata together with
41:    the corresponding classified hook. Preserve rejection of arbitrary attributes
42:    and unsupported grid bindings. Native grid dispatch already routes ENHANCE to
43:    the materialized mesh because its read-only field has no grid source; retain
44:    that geometric-domain fallback rather than accepting a zero field.
45: 5. Validate all scalars, metadata, command membership, hook requirements and
46:    transaction identity before allocating attributes, changing topology state,
47:    building caches or capturing undo. Restore command working settings on every
48:    exit. Keep FEATURE_ALIGN, general authored attributes, anchored/preview and
49:    dyntopo admission guarded until their own implementation gates pass.
50: 
51: ## Acceptance
52: 
53: - Native preparation verifies authoritative settings, independent command values,
54:   static-stack rejection, exact types, range normalization and failure atomicity.
55: - Multi-leaf live/CSR-configured mesh tests run two ENHANCE commands with different
56:   rings/inner in both orders, intervening DRAW, repeated dabs, zero/missed first
57:   dabs, changed pressure on strength, growth of support and nonaccumulation.
58:   Compare with an independent original-formula oracle and separately configured
59:   raw single-command references where their first-contact regions coincide.
60: - Prove distinct command cache keys, reusable unchanged keys, correct first-contact
61:   values after geometry changes, and no premature capture caused by a larger later
62:   command. Include an unbounded later command to force all-leaf selection.
63: - Exercise topology generation changes, stroke reset, undo/redo, absent temporary
64:   attribute allocation, failed late commands, validate-only and zero-count batches.
65:   Reuse the existing transaction/owner lifetime validation without weakening it.
66: - Actual Python/DLL and installed headed tests must require prepared execution on
67:   the selected mesh domain, including materialized multires fallback. Verify the
68:   fallback selects the correct domain before mutation and still uses prepared
69:   properties; raw fallback cannot stand in for generic property support.
70: - Regenerate bindings, run applicable native suites and package provenance checks.
71:   Record source reviews, corrections, commands and artifact identities before
72:   checking this capability off in the Plan 6 task list.
73: 
74: ## Review questions
75: 
76: The reviews must resolve whether writing the active values into the existing TEMP
77: attribute preserves attribute binding and lifetime rules, whether raw first-contact
78: semantics require a different support/cache policy, and how the mesh fallback for
79: multires interacts with the existing prepared owner/transaction validation. These
80: are implementation questions; no user approval is required for routine corrections.
